# Citation metadata (https://citation-file-format.github.io/). GitHub shows it as
# "Cite this repository" on the repository page, and Zenodo reads it when a release is
# archived there. To be cited by name, replace the entity below with a person:
#   - family-names: "<family name>"
#     given-names: "<given names>"
#     orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
# and add the DOI Zenodo assigns under `identifiers`.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Tabsmith in research, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Tabsmith: train a model from a CSV and ship it inside a Rust application"
abstract: >-
  Tabsmith trains tabular models (linear, small MLP, gradient-boosted trees) from a
  CSV or Parquet file with one command and ships them without Python: as a verified
  bundle loaded by a small runtime crate, or compiled into typed no_std Rust code whose
  prediction the Kani model checker proves panic-free, with sound output bounds and
  monotone constraints checked at compile time. Every bundle carries conformal
  prediction intervals or sets and permutation importance.
authors:
  - name: "Tabsmith contributors"
version: 0.3.0
date-released: 2026-11-01
license:
  - MIT
  - Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/NatMartini/Tabsmith"
url: "https://github.com/NatMartini/Tabsmith"
keywords:
  - rust
  - machine-learning
  - tabular-data
  - gradient-boosting
  - model-deployment
  - no-std
  - embedded
  - formal-verification
  - kani
  - conformal-prediction
